| F_pl | Power-law value function family of perpetual American options |
| F_V | Density family implied by pricing system V ∈ {v2, curve, new} |
| q* | Realized (empirical) density of the underlying |
| L_V(φ) | Kurtosis-arb extraction operator against system V |
| d(q*, F) | Mismatch distance; inf over q ∈ F of ││q* − q││ |

### **At-The-Limit Equivalence Of Conservation Law With “Change ‘w’ To Skew Curve So Proforma Post-Trade Point****'****s Slope, Migrates To Pre-Trade Point”**

Conventional Balancer mechanic (View A): a swap (Δx, Δy) keeps k = x^w · y^(1−w) and w fixed; reserves move along the curve from (x, y) to (x', y'). The marginal price at the post-trade point is p' = (w/(1−w)) · (y'/x').

Our mechanic (View B): the conservation law fixes α = x · w and β = y · (1−w). A swap moves (x, y, w) simultaneously, with w derived as w = α/x at any state. Reachable states satisfy α/x + β/y = 1, i.e. the hyperbola (x − α)(y − β) = αβ.

Marginal price under View B: p = (w/(1−w)) · (y/x) = (α/x)/(β/y) · (y/x) = α y² / (β x²).

At-the-limit equivalence: differentiate p along the trade trajectory under View B and along the constant-k curve under View A; to leading order in trade size, dp/dx agree. Hence for infinitesimal trades the two mechanics produce identical price impact at the pre-trade point.

### **Derivation Of Continuous Case From Discrete Step Conservation Law**

From the conservation law, w · x = α and (1−w) · y = β are constants along any trade trajectory. Together with w + (1−w) = 1, this gives α/x + β/y = 1, equivalent to (x − α)(y − β) = αβ — a hyperbola in the (x, y) plane with asymptotes x = α and y = β.

Parametrise the trajectory by x: y(x) = β + αβ/(x − α). The marginal price along the trajectory is p(x) = α y(x)² / (β x²).

For a continuous trade taking the reserves from (x₀, y₀) to (x₁, y₁) along the hyperbola, the cash leg integrates to:

    Δy = y(x₁) − y(x₀) = αβ · [ 1/(x₁ − α) − 1/(x₀ − α) ].

Closing the discrete-to-continuous bridge: this expression is recovered as the integral of p(x) dx along the trajectory, by direct substitution.

### **No Round-Trip Math**

A round trip is a trade from (x₀, y₀) to (x₁, y₁) followed by the reverse trade back to (x₀, y₀). By the conservation law, (α, β) are invariant under both legs. Marginal price p = α y² / (β x²) is a function of (x, y) alone — it returns to its initial value at (x₀, y₀).

Cash and underlying balances return to their original values, since the reserves do. Hence no path-dependent leakage on round trips, beyond protocol fees applied per leg.

### **Formal Verification (Lean 4): Kurtosis-Arb Rigidity Bound**

Setting. Three rigid pricing systems V ∈ {v2, curve, new}, each with implied density q_V and parametric family F_V. A kurtosis-arb portfolio φ has bounded sup-norm |φ|_∞ ≤ M. The extraction operator L_V(φ) quantifies cash extractable via φ against system V. The mismatch distance d(q*, F_V) = inf over q ∈ F_V of ||q* − q||.

Hypothesis (FamilyPolySpan). For each system V, any two densities q₁, q₂ ∈ F_V satisfy q₁ − q₂ ∈ span{1, K, K²} a.e. — i.e. each family is parametrised by mean / variance / gamma alone.

Lemma 1 (Universal Bound). |L_V(φ)| ≤ M · d(q*, F_V) for any kurtosis-arb portfolio φ with |φ|_∞ ≤ M.

Lemma 2 (Family Alignment). q* ∈ F_new ⇒ d(q*, F_new) = 0. Moreover F_new ⊄ F_v2 and F_new ⊄ F_curve.

Main Theorem. In the natural regime q* ∈ F_new (= F_pl, the power-law family), |L_new(φ)| = 0 ≤ |L_V(φ)| for any other rigid system V ∈ {v2, curve}.

Verification status. Aristotle (Lean 4 / Mathlib) closes the proof with zero sorries and only standard axioms (propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound).

Bridging Remark. FamilyPolySpan is an idealisation. Real families {lognormal, StableSwap, power-law} satisfy it only to leading order: first-order Taylor expansions in family parameters live in span{1, K, K²}, but the families themselves do not literally satisfy the polynomial-difference condition pointwise. Higher-order corrections are bounded and do not change the qualitative ordering d(q*, F_new) < d(q*, F_v2), d(q*, F_curve) in natural regimes. Stating the hypothesis explicitly is preferable to burying the idealisation in prose: anyone scrutinising the result can point to one definition and ask whether the real family satisfies it; the honest answer is yes to leading order, with bounded higher-order corrections.

Out of scope of this verification: time-integration / persistence of extraction; cross-venue capital constraints; funding countercyclicality as an additive LP-protection mechanism; flexible (non-rigid) systems such as Univ3 / Panoptic.

